{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e1f3255f450e4761e20b64c0782a241a7d73637c9e276fa9c1e07227f26cf4e2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-162706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-3.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-3.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ and $(x,y)=(1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1d257445551687ded71409efdce1b8695a9813a73ecb0fde5b6f4efccc6c15be.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-162706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"qu