type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$A,$ $C,$ and $D$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ and $D$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/c460920f6b2516ac8603852e12d15840cca051736555ea43cf15a278ef9e0155.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $C$ and $D.$ Hence $C$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/cc526bb1cf73fb5b5579966e91077ccd096aea475a4cf13196166bd65ac88d7b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-164152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ and $D$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"] ["text" "$D$ only"] ["text" "The function does not have local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$C$ and $D$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $C.$ Hence, $B$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $B({x}_{B},{y}_{B},{z}_{B}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{B},{y}_{B})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $B$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/6d98be34c848e0fbce571fc572fe9dae244e6ce80b677063fea2e19983e1a2da.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-164162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $D$ only"] ["text" "$B,$ $C,$ and $D$ only"] ["text" "$C$ and $D$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-164162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2-\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2+\\sqrt{2},$ what type of point is $a?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-2<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-2-\\sqrt{2}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =-2+\\sqrt{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "inconclusive"] ["text" "local maximum"] ["text" "local minimum"] ["text" "not a critical point"] ["text" "saddle point"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "local maximum"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=\\sqrt{6},$ what type of point is $a?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-2<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-\\sqrt{6}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =\\sqrt{6}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "inconclusive"] ["text" "local maximum"] ["text" "local minimum"] ["text" "not a critical point"] ["text" "saddle point"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "saddle point"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -12\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,{\\lambda }_{2}=-16$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-16,$ what type of point is $a?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-4<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-16<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =-16<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "inconclusive"] ["text" "local maximum"] ["text" "local minimum"] ["text" "not a critical point"] ["text" "saddle point"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "local maximum"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,{\\lambda }_{2}=3-\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3+\\sqrt{2},$ what type of point is $a?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =8>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3-\\sqrt{2}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =3+\\sqrt{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "inconclusive"] ["text" "local maximum"] ["text" "local minimum"] ["text" "not a critical point"] ["text" "saddle point"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "local minimum"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "inconclusive"] ["text" "local maximum"] ["text" "local minimum"] ["text" "not a critical point"] ["text" "saddle point"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "local maximum"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-9+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|=20>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=40>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=4$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309