on/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4309a945f72371e7a242587777f8b50b6ab3b7b25f7720ea0a9f56c7ec316590"} {"question" "q-163789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "q-163789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/state.json" "file_sha256" "7793ca9ea7c44f974e5beda47bcbdd1871701d8f695074cc29af48bc3f5f5f4a"} {"question" "e-12460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b550d203e3be327eb2af27a1035b6693953dd2802bb203af06d5773e48d609c3.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-12460.json" "file_sha256" "eb4d0e3bfe9d59f8d8bcf08361f08883d0550848c61e31b09160f6780c48c2d2"} {"question" "e-12460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y) = (0,-2),$ so the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f80d19126252f6ab7097cf528aec9726c2708f48c815c026f632fccd77cf74d4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-12460.json" "file_sha256" "eb4d0e3bfe9d59f8d8bcf08361f08883d0550848c61e31b09160f6780c48c2d2"} {"question" "e-12461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/6c6906429cf946f196b959cce7c20589b6212ae45df9607e15c6385be9aef858.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-12461.json" "file_sha256" "06cc943b3fbaf0172f1fd921ec67d93dc83689a2171c2ba71b28350e1eb6163e"} {"question" "e-12461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B,$ and $D.$ Hence, $A, B,$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/cdb83ac891799788c8289f7e44902bf6be008cb13a7bf27f330b48113df021d1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-12461.json" "file_sha256" "06cc943b3fbaf0172f1fd921ec67d93dc83689a2171c2ba71b28350e1eb6163e"} {"question" "e-12465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/2ae5b42ae953d1b9d3fd642e9938924a9b2bc439dee1ea52c868f186798b1520.png)\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-12465.json" "file_sha256" "bf8c39a05f0d386645258f6a87e49bdde65983ab241b4ce66147bd6d2524db73"} {"question" "e-12465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n+\n\\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ Hence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-12465.json" "file_sha256" "bf8c39a05f0d386645258f6a87e49bdde65983ab241b4ce66147bd6d2524db73"} {"question" "e-7030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e21f1a654e7ec74a637369e2330d0e0f3ccd0a3469bc15405b9ae581aaa4efba.png) about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e2a3ebddc9b6755b15eca1e5497dfc859ea5c2d6bfb5ee0a98df1c6476f87a4f.png) is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a21655b6405345e52c0f18c50e098409ca06e2b938ee77dc1b839c54f5f60456.png)\n\nwhere \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a963b613e053f027bc0434bacaba0056c70493b72181eff33f97cd16d39afc37.png) \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8317d25a4b5a486cf86db32d0868ca8135c4701abf2e2f579b59ce7f4c3ec05b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/5db28cc61ee0aadd0ccc482cacf12c3a851ff803af7927d3bb918bc9c7c72ffc.png)\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8f3cbdf2ea946537b9792aba2720fb22db787b8b72be93a3c0a5308ff111ee13.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/42f776502d5c552cefc008f7ea701f0f45578326e649b3f4dcd4bc548738d0ef.png) is a saddle point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/fb7b36c5e6bd7e8c334d8874eb7a781085930a078aadd1cabc79475006fa894c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-7030.json" "file_sha256" "a7f6b050fb06f3b212182a28722d80815fe71156949e6793b523532cd5d32835"} {"question" "e-7030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/7ac5ab8e5444901eb7e978a0916e953bbe6da7ff8fb8a481537ad1f07d5bf48d.png) is a critical point of the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/87f22c24143e0fdad241f6b070b0e0ba1d89b2d5c32669989a7c75df700f5e75.png) if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/2fa6429606f96f4c4f43b91de76acab79152fd9989108836084cea77e0d9db09.png) In such cases, we can classify ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1287cfe4aa57a8ec4c01f84934cb29b3d524f0524a48e39d7f6a30aed97e8ee2.png) as follows:\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is positive definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a local minimum\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/91ecc7d66921c91ed5baf1b1d2453f9cc4678feed21f325291b38f103f0266b6.png) is negative definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/74bbb71a684ae2b42d8a3779704a0c8b47ef43e5cebd265861418f77cd2b6267.png) is a local maximum\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1ada0c06e92bc2e76f33fade0b4f28728f641cc98923f855735c118d2ea0ba25.png) Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/29aa6115eeac65ed8ca2194e8fdce4de48591b144de9ee298e7c151f78bda4ac.png) for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/ffb48b96eb46bc28ce8546552edbc23496ae84720dc2cc4413cc7425b056bba0.png)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/363026acf3cf01ffee6edd4166c849b1cce0cfd1e14af8dc3a15fa2a2f41163d.png) matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/50946bb3afa72276eb132efe89bbe450df8836747fa6d11d04dbb142da7f6fad.png) Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/407d3bd58712c649860e47e39322f4471da8446ed312741900b2697fafe9d4a5.png) (as well as the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/849d81b747384fa1c4d5194327dd049207fdb7bf2db258e117c7214f870555b3.png)) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a93d47764597bd39da3f5bb625a6d1977c61f6a731e8b041850fef5dc9c7bc52.png) for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8161c7c2b38d84c7033c688b3b41d0decc2861cd218f08dc3f87625eed2cc376.png)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/127d0aace5f471d897e0b6171e68da877234f9b709bf379245032522de9f6aa5.png) we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b18d3603f870bb527a4ee4618ed482165e62659a001cf8f2bbef4503c249b313.png) when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/541f4f6d68d831274235815dceef666c2a59172ab8825d63aa189957530d9ce9.png) is odd, and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/32a94e6ea1436d78a428c8ff22e50a7df311609ab74824c672abd2334c83988a.png) when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/aebdc0a3ecd72c42a452c68c6019eff7ad41de49b8a09517f7fd2ff8df58e32f.png) is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/fcb57eaada70434abbf4a6056c9a8a793f6a0348196813e6a512de4772290f8e.png) but\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/ae6614c135f2e9d1d011de5f58d17fb4e43c2c6b87a7831a57b1287cf1a6e13d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1851f51854993b0360e75b0fc717263a7a84938b86ae515d74be32a0ac87362d.png)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/7906e975aaeffa8eae6d319b4be2d6a585c41b95ec59d721eedb7355fda29066.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) is a saddle point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/4f118f558380017fb67aa8f0b030dfa06a10216b7381582c619907c32988f211.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/example-7030.json" "file_sha256" "a7f6b050fb06f3b212182a28722d80815fe71156949e6793b523532cd5d32835"} {"question" "q-162747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $D$ only"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "e1d0ed7b774c6cf2b488c485a159a5d3b3a52f7e01dffac10eeb1d728ffb21fb" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "A is the bottom of the depression. D has a neighborhood whose values are equal to or greater than its value, so it qualifies under the non-strict definition of local minimum. B lies on a slope and has nearby lower values; C is a maximum. The authentic worked solution explicitly confirms A and D. Saved grading marks the submitted A and B only Incorrect, consistent with this derived judgment rather than a direct answer-key capture." "bindings" {"problem" "d2d2b9ec607907a6fe0e1c29cb9ef0c2d6b56bef8a79769e2621fb97f5929692" "worked_solution" "67dfa426c9df05a3c0decd1c07c05ae9a096f5c9015d30a7b4e34c8c04c931ea" "original_field" "883852eb39e86b46f7b866d44fc62982d15844db9ea092315df57535c44dedb9" "reviewed_field" "38b406cd3fe7db9794f889222e4e62b048be906ade4a6e7f532fb16e9307fa55" "raw_widget" "9a6173c12481fb4d56adfddd27d0b651655cb6679af9e673b3ceb788dbe31e36"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-162747-before.png" "sha256" "d9c77b34b8023f869545f3019050a9a79374121774647191320c26ad851c732f"} {"path" "history-q-162747.png" "sha256" "2bcab350b2a64d4a5a77571a5f9d36b2428404e6bf6640a3e655d0dda402d246"} {"path" "q-162747-after.json" "sha256" "59dd23c4a4192dea9296b97cfd48340fb7d12b46b4642af3d125f3a8df4b004a"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "33cc15f2f2df9c13791d3148c07006dfe8776816d5636d4ab74cf5cb094838fb"}]} {"question" "q-162747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $D$ only"] ["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $D$ only"] ["text" "$D$ only"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "e1d0ed7b774c6cf2b488c485a159a5d3b3a52f7e01dffac10eeb1d728ffb21fb" "rationale" "A is the bottom of the depression. D has a neighborhood whose values are equal to or greater than its value, so it qualifies under the non-strict definition of local minimum. B lies on a slope and has nearby lower values; C is a maximum. The authentic worked solution explicitly confirms A and D. Saved grading marks the submitted A and B only Incorrect, consistent with this derived judgment rather than a direct answer-key capture."}] "usage" {"e-12465" [] "q-163787" [] "e-7030" [] "e-12460" [] "q-162706" [] "q-162747" [] "q-163723" [] "e-12461" []} "basis" 2492 "retractions" [[17592186124730 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/q-162706.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?"] [17592186124732 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/q-162747.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?"] [17592186124732 :question/answer-fields 17592186121411] [17592186124741 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{3}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 3 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x - a)$.\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $f^{″}(a)$ are $λ_{1} =-2, λ_{2} =-\\sqrt{6}$ and $λ_{3} = \\sqrt{6}$, what type of point is $a$?"] [17592186124740 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{3}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 9 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}A(x - a)$,\n\nwhere\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} = 6$ and $det(A) > 0$\n2. $A$ is positive-definite\n3. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$"] [17592186124127 :question/problem "The graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/5e11926055cd53b797f7b551db802dde.png)"] [17592186124127 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum is $2$. The function reaches this value when\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (0,-2)\n$$\n\nso the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/03dac4b5188cba2c29ae834a68a2ac47.png)"] [17592186124128 :question/problem "The graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/6ed81efec74ea191b65f65cf5cbf32bd.png)"] [17592186124128 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)\n$$\n\nfor all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b)$.\n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B$, and $D$. Hence, $A, B$, and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D)$, there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that\n\n$$\nf(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nHowever, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)\n$$\n\n, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/95fe3bf8748bc789ab6a58b3ea36f6e1.png)"] [17592186124129 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3$, what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}$?"] [17592186124129 :question/worked-solution "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\ngiven by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) (\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f$.\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= -1 < 0 \\\\\nλ_{2} &= -3 < 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f$."] [17592186124297 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is given by\n$f(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx 2 + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T A (\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$,\n\nwhere\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} = 1$ and $\\det(A) = -4$\n2. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n3. $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point of $f$"] [17592186124297 :question/worked-solution "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nNow, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet $\\Delta_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\ndenote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A$. Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$,\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $a_{11} = 1$, but\n$det(A)|=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = 1(0) - 1(2); =-2$.\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n$Δ_{1}|= a_{11} = 1 > 0; Δ_{2}|= \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2|= 1 > 0; Δ_{3}|= \\mid A|=-2 < 0$\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point of $f$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121400 17592186121401 17592186121402 17592186121403 17592186121404 17592186121412 175921